{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-10-direct-variation",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Direct Variation",
    "description": "Turn direct proportionality into a constant-multiplier equation and extend it to powers, roots, and shifted quantities.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use a “same multiplier” picture first, then name the constant k. Move from y∝x to powers and roots, and show that comparing two cases often lets the constant cancel.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach direct variation x ∝ y -> x=ky and how to determine k from one known pair. Extend to y ∝ x^2, y ∝ x^3, y ∝ sqrt(x), and y ∝ (x+c). Include physical-style applications from the chapter such as pendulum time versus square root of length and value of a diamond versus a power of weight/volume. Show how ratios often eliminate k entirely when comparing two cases."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Direct variation means one quantity follows the same underlying multiplier as another.\n\nWe’ll turn that idea into a constant-k relation and then extend it to squares, cubes, roots, and shifted quantities from the chapter’s exam patterns.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If y varies directly as x, doubling x doubles y.\n\nWrite y = kx. The number k tells how much y there is per unit of x.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If y=15 when x=6, then k=15/6=2.5.\nSo the relation is y=2.5x.\n\nNow any new x gives its matching y.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
